# Why this patch exists:
# Upstream bug in @sumup/medusa-plugin@0.1.0 — its toMajorUnitNumber() helper
# is named as if it converts Medusa's minor-unit amounts (e.g. pence) to the
# major units SumUp's API expects (e.g. pounds), but it never actually
# divided by anything — it just coerced Medusa's BigNumber-shaped amount into
# a plain JS number and returned it unchanged. SumUp's own SDK documents
# `amount` as "expressed in major units", so every checkout and refund this
# plugin created was submitted at 100x the intended charge (e.g. a £11.00
# cart total was sent to SumUp as 1100, which SumUp records as £1,100.00).
# Confirmed directly against SumUp's API: GET on a created checkout showed
# "amount": 1100 for an intended £11.00 charge.
#
# Fix: toMajorUnitNumber() now divides by the currency's own decimal-digit
# factor (via @medusajs/framework/utils's defaultCurrencies), not a blind
# /100 — currencies like JPY/CLP have 0 decimal places and would be wrong
# under a hardcoded divisor. Applied at all three call sites that funnel
# through this same broken helper: checkout creation
# (providers/sumup/utils.js, createCheckoutPayload), refunds
# (providers/sumup/service.js, refundPayment), and the cart-total-changed
# reconciliation path (providers/sumup/service.js, updatePayment) — the last
# of these was missed in the first pass of this patch and added afterward;
# it only looked correct before because GBP's 2-decimal default happened to
# match the unpatched fallback.
#
# No upstream issue filed yet as of writing — worth reporting to the
# plugin's GitHub repo (github.com/sumup/medusa-plugin-sumup) when there's
# time (not urgent, but this is a payment-correctness bug, not cosmetic).
# Safe to delete this patch (and its entry in pnpm-workspace.yaml /
# pnpm-lock.yaml) once upstream ships a fix and the dependency is upgraded
# past it.
